title: Target C++ Projects

files:
  - .assets/img-103-01-02.png
  - .assets/img-103-02-01.png
  - .assets/img-103-02-02.png
  - .assets/img-103-02-03.png
  - .assets/img-103-02-04.png

steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 1
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;Working Backwards}

      To fully grasp the importance of {[cu]BuildC}, we'll first exam&shy;ine the _output_ of the **EMS** program build process.

      Using a minimal program supported by a skeletal distro, we'll visit generated files found the `.emscript` folder {[dc,4]}.

      Viewed together, the contents of `.emscript` represent a standalone C++ project featuring a familiar set of artifacts.

      <div class="em-info">

      Some of these artifacts [`main.cpp`] originate within the **EMS** translator; others come (in-)directly from {[cu]BuildC}.
      </div>

      So let's proceed to populate the `.emscript` project&thinsp;&hellip;


  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;Minimal Target Program}

      This picture captures the steps required to build virtually empty {[cu]Gist00} pro&shy;gram targeting a skeletal  **EMS** distro.

      {[DC,1]} select {[em]Board &ndash; &lt;bare-metal&gt;}<br>
      {[DC,2]} select {[em]Setup &ndash; segger.arm.sandbox://default}<br>
      {[DC,3]} locate `segger.arm.gists.sandbox/Gist00.em.ts`<br>
      {[DC,4]} click the **Build&thinsp;+&thinsp;Load** button and track progress<br>

      <div class="em-info">

      We encourage you to "play along at home" by simply following these same steps in your local environment.

      </div>

      The **OUTPUT** panel shows status of the **EMS** translator {[dc,5]}, the backend C++ compiler {[dc,6]}, and the program loader {[dc,7]}.

      Note that the executable image corresponding to {[cu]Gist00} weighs in at just 156 bytes &ndash; truly tiny code&thinsp;!!

  - cmds:
      - view 3
    text: |
      {[ht,$build]&thinsp;Generated C++ Project}

      To best understand the role of {[cu]BuildC}, we'll start by in&shy;specting the special `workspace/.emscript` folder.

      Shown as **EM&bull;Script Output** in **Content** view, this fold&shy;er houses (generated) artifacts of a standalone C++ project.

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} `main.cpp` &ndash; the program source itself<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} `build.sh` &ndash; a script to compile the program<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} `linkcmd.ld` the program's memory placement<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} `load.sh` &ndash; a script to load the executable program<br>

      The `.out` sub-folder then contains a typical set of artifacts produced by the underlying build tools.

      <div class="em-info">

      If you try the right-click **Clean&thinsp;&hellip;** command, remem&shy;ber to  **Build&thinsp;+&thinsp;Load** the {[cu]Gist00} program before proceeding.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 4
    text: |
      {[ht,conversion_path]&thinsp;From %[cu]Gist00% to <code>main.cpp</code>}

      Passing `segger.arm.sandbox/Gist00.em.ts` to the **EMS** translator generates a corresponding `main.cpp` program.

      Lines labeled {[bm,G]}&thinsp; in `main.cpp` directly reference the top-level {[cu]Gist00} module &ndash; its header, body, and entry point.

      <div class="em-info">

      But how about the rest of `main.cpp`&hellip;&nbsp; What prompts **EMS** to generate these extra {[ck]#include} directives&thinsp;??

      </div>

      In short, the current distro's {[cu]BuildC} composite &ndash; which the **EMS** translator _automatically_ imports into the mix.

      <div class="em-info">

      Had our {[cu]Gist00} program explicitly {[ck]import}'d other **EMS** modules, these would appear inside `main.cpp` as well.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 5
    text: |
      {[ht,playground]&thinsp;A Distro Playground}

      As a rule, _every_&thinsp; **EMS** distro package will contain a {[cu]BuildC} com&shy;posite &ndash; including the skeletal distro illustrated here.

      Stripped to its essentials, `segger.arm.sandbox` provides a "playground" for disecting the **EMS** build flow in isolation.

      <div class="em-info">

      Unlike other distros, our <code>..sandbox</code> distro lacks its own {[cu]BoardC} for configuring a target MCU platform.

      </div>

      Our `..sandbox` {[cu]BuildC} employs an ARM C/C++ com&shy;piler from [SEGGER](https://doc.segger.com/UM20007_Compiler.html) already used within other **EMS** distros.

      So let's finally visit {[cu]BoardC} &ndash; as well as explore other source files found in our distro playground.
